<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-distance" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Distance</title>

  <p>
    We denote the set of all real numbers<fn>Undefined term</fn> by <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-distance">
    <statement>
      <p>
        We denote the <term>distance</term><fn>Undefined term</fn> between the points
        <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> by <m>|AB|</m>. We define the <term>length</term> of the
        segment <m>[AB]</m> to be <m>|AB|</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We often denote the lengths of the three sides of a triangle by <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>,
        and <m>c</m>. The usual thing for a triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m> is to take
        <m>a = |BC|</m>, i.e. the length of the side opposite the vertex <m>A</m>, and
        similarly <m>b = |CA|</m> and <m>c = |AB|</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-triangle-sides">
    <caption>Triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m> with side lengths <m>a = |BC|</m>, <m>b = |CA|</m>, <m>c = |AB|</m></caption>
    <image width="45%">
      <description>A triangle with vertices A, B, C and side lengths labelled a, b, c opposite the respective vertices</description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (3,0) -- (1.5,2.5) -- cycle;
          \filldraw (0,0)     circle (2pt) node[below left]  {$A$};
          \filldraw (3,0)     circle (2pt) node[below right] {$B$};
          \filldraw (1.5,2.5) circle (2pt) node[above]       {$C$};
          \node at (1.5,-0.35) {$c = |AB|$};
          \node at (2.55,1.35) {$a = |BC|$};
          \node at (0.45,1.35) {$b = |CA|$};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <axiom xml:id="ax-ruler">
    <title>Ruler Axiom</title>
    <statement>
      <p>The distance between points has the following properties:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>the distance <m>|AB|</m> is never negative;</p></li>
        <li><p><m>|AB| = |BA|</m>;</p></li>
        <li><p>if <m>C</m> lies on <m>AB</m>, between <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, then
          <m>|AB| = |AC| + |CB|</m>;</p></li>
        <li><p>(marking off a distance) given any ray from <m>A</m>, and given any real
          number <m>k \geq 0</m>, there is a unique point <m>B</m> on the ray whose
          distance from <m>A</m> is <m>k</m>.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </axiom>

  <definition xml:id="def-midpoint">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>midpoint</term> of the segment <m>[AB]</m> is the point <m>M</m> of the
        segment with<fn>Students may notice that the first equality implies the second.</fn>
        <me>|AM| = |MB| = \frac{|AB|}{2}.</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-midpoint">
    <caption>The midpoint <m>M</m> of segment <m>[AB]</m>, with <m>|AM| = |MB|</m></caption>
    <image width="60%">
      <description>A line segment from A to B with midpoint M marked at the centre, with tick marks showing the two halves are equal</description>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (4,0);
          \filldraw (0,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$A$};
          \filldraw (2,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$M$};
          \filldraw (4,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$B$};
          \draw (0.9,0.12) -- (1.1,-0.12);
          \draw (2.9,0.12) -- (3.1,-0.12);
          \node[above] at (1,0.1) {$|AM|$};
          \node[above] at (3,0.1) {$|MB|$};
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercises xml:id="exs-distance">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-dist-midpoint">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The segment <m>[AB]</m> has length <m>|AB| = 10</m> cm. The point <m>M</m>
          is the midpoint. Find <m>|AM|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>The midpoint divides the segment into two equal halves.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p><m>|AM| = 5</m> cm.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>By the definition of midpoint,
        <m>|AM| = \frac{|AB|}{2} = \frac{10}{2} = 5</m> cm.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-dist-ruler">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Points <m>A</m>, <m>C</m>, <m>B</m> lie on a line in that order, with
          <m>C</m> between <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. If <m>|AC| = 4</m> and
          <m>|CB| = 7</m>, find <m>|AB|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Use property (iii) of the <xref ref="ax-ruler"/>.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p><m>|AB| = 11</m>.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>Since <m>C</m> lies between <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, the Ruler Axiom gives
        <m>|AB| = |AC| + |CB| = 4 + 7 = 11</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-dist-sides">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m>, use the standard notation to write the
          length of each side in terms of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>Each lowercase letter names the side opposite the matching uppercase vertex.</p>
      </hint>
      <answer>
        <p><m>a = |BC|</m>, <m>b = |CA|</m>, <m>c = |AB|</m>.</p>
      </answer>
      <solution>
        <p>By convention, <m>a = |BC|</m> is the side opposite <m>A</m>,
        <m>b = |CA|</m> is opposite <m>B</m>, and <m>c = |AB|</m> is opposite <m>C</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>

</section>
